<p>At the height of their productivity as Beatles, neither Paul McCartney nor John Lennon could read or write music notation. Clearly it didn’t keep them from making a cultural impact, and as with so much else in life, they regarded it humorously. As McCartney has said, they used to liken themselves to ancient Egyptian pharaohs, who relied on scribes rather than being literate themselves. It’s worth considering whether, all things considered, this inability was really a deficiency: not being rigorously schooled in the traditions and conventions of music could have given them just the freedom they needed to create fresh songs. And if you’ve struggled with procrastination, have you considered likening yourself to Leonardo da Vinci?</p>
<p>“Leonardo’s difficulties with sticking to tasks were pervasive from childhood,” says <a href="https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2019/05/190523202604.htm">a ScienceDaily summary of a 2019 paper</a> by clinical neuroscientist Marco Catani. “<span style="font-size: 13pt;">Accounts from biographers and contemporaries show Leonardo was constantly on the go, often jumping from task to task.” </span>Regarding his life at a distance of half a millennium, we may consider Leonardo not just a genius but a highly productive one, with a body of work including numerous <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2021/04/the-ingenious-inventions-of-leonardo-da-vinci-recreated-with-3d-animation.html">inventions built and unbuilt</a>&nbsp;as well as paintings like <em>Lady with an Ermine</em>,<em> The Last Supper</em>, and the&nbsp;<em><a href="https://www.openculture.com/2025/12/see-what-the-original-mona-lisa-likely-looked-like.html">Mona Lisa</a>&nbsp;</em>— which nearly joined the man’s pile of abandoned projects. As Nicolas Cole writes at&nbsp;<em>Inc</em><em>.</em>, scholarship suggests that Leonardo “painted the Mona Lisa on and off for a few years starting in 1503, left it unfinished, and didn’t complete it until close to his death in 1519.”</p>
