</div>

<p>“While painting <em>The Last Supper</em>, Leonardo would sometimes stare at the work for an hour, finally make one small stroke, and then leave,” writes Walter Isaacson in his biography <a href="https://amzn.to/4dgIGSK"><em>Leonardo da Vinci</em></a>. His explanation: “Men of lofty genius sometimes accomplish the most when they work least, for their minds are occupied with their ideas and the perfection of their conceptions, to which they afterwards give form.” When the non-Renaissance men of the world say things like that, it comes off as self-aggrandizing excuse-making. Many, perhaps most of us have spent so much time telling ourselves we’re preparing for a task that it just became another means of procrastination. But “procrastinating like Leonardo requires work: it involves gathering all the possible facts and ideas, and only after that allowing the collection to simmer,” notes Isaacson.</p>
<p>Not that the patrons frustrated by Leonardo’s failures to deliver could always keep that in mind. After he worked at the Vatican for three years, Pope Leo X dismissed him with the judgment that he would never accomplish anything, “for he begins by thinking of the end of the work, before the beginning.” Even Leonardo himself made a deathbed apology “to God and Man for leaving so much undone.” Having examined the nature of both Leonardo’s challenges and his brilliance, Catani comes to the conclusion that he exhibited traits consistent with a condition <span style="font-size: 13pt;">only given a name well within living memory, </span><span style="font-size: 13pt;">Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD). </span>When we find ourselves compulsively fleeing from the incomplete work at hand, we’d do well to bear in mind not just Leonardo’s example, but also <a href="https://x.com/ahaspel/status/186097074817859586">the words of aphorist Aaron Haspel</a>: “We all suffer from a cluster of psychological disorders; it used to be known as personality.”</p>
